title": "Duplicates: 25 rows removed", "severity": "low", "description": "25 duplicate records (the same ID twice) were removed before the analysis.", "affected_metric": "", "suggested_investigation": "Find where duplicate records enter the export."}], "proactive_questions": ["Why did Segment B's units rise 41.5% month-over-month while its revenue fell 12.6% \u2014 is average order value declining within that segment?", "Is the recent spike in discounts concentrated in a particular region or segment, and does it explain the short-term revenue drop?", "Does the association between revenue and Marketing Spend hold within each segment separately, or is it driven by one segment's scale?"], "methodology_notes": "Cleaning (cleaning rules-v1): Removed 25 duplicate rows. Outliers flagged (kept) in: revenue. More than 40% missing in: notes \u2014 review recommended. Final dataset: 3000 rows (started with 3025). Analysis (analysis v7) computed: anomaly, correlation, eda, segment, test, trend. Correlations are Pearson r: linear association only, never cause. Descriptive statistics use the non-missing values of each column. Two-group differences are tested with Welch's t-test. Month-over-month compares calendar-month totals (means for rates and 0-1 flags).", "executive_view": {"headline": "This 12-month order dataset covers 3,000 orders across three customer segments and four regions, with revenue moving together with Marketing Spend but showing a recent short-term decline.", "headline_evidence_ids": ["clean:audit", "corr:revenue__marketing_spend", "trend:revenue:7d_rolling"], "paragraphs": [{"text": "Segment A leads on average revenue, while the most recent 7-day window shows revenue and units both falling even as discounts are rising.", "evidence_ids": ["seg:segment/revenue", "trend:revenue:7d_rolling", "trend:units:7d_rolling", "trend:discount:7d_rolling"]}, {"text": "Segment B's revenue fell sharply month-over-month while Segment C's rose, so the overall picture masks meaningful divergence across segments.", "evidence_ids": ["trend:revenue (B):MoM", "trend:revenue (C):MoM"]}], "findings": [{"text": "Segment A averages $121.29 in revenue per order, Segment B $98.06, and Segment C $78.88, with Segment A also the largest group at 1,212 orders.", "evidence_ids": ["seg:segment/revenue", "seg:segment/revenue:count"]}, {"text": "Revenue and Marketing Spend move together across orders, while revenue shows little to no association with units or discount.", "evidence_ids": ["corr:revenue__marketing_spend", "corr:revenue__units", "corr:revenue__discount"]}, {"text": "In the most recent 7-day window, revenue is down 19.9%, units down 12.4%, and Marketing Spend down 8.7%, while discounts are up 23.4%.", "evidence_ids": ["trend:revenue:7d_rolling", "trend:units:7d_rolling", "trend:marketing_spend:7d_rolling", "trend:discount:7d_rolling"]}, {"text": "Comparing Oct 2026 with Sep 2026, Segment B revenue fell 12.6% while Segment C rose 6.5% and Segment A fell 1.8%; overall revenue was down 2.8%.", "evidence_ids": ["trend:revenue (B):MoM", "trend:revenue (C):MoM", "trend:revenue (A):MoM", "trend:revenue:MoM"]}], "action_items": [{"action": "Investigate the short-term revenue and Marketing Spend decline alongside the spike in discounts to determine whether discounting is offsetting spend cuts or masking a demand drop.", "expected_impact": "Prevents misreading a potential structural issue as a routine fluctuation.", "evidence_ids": ["trend:revenue:7d_rolling", "trend:discount:7d_rolling", "trend:marketing_spend:7d_rolling"]}, {"action": "Examine Segment B's October order mix and pricing to understand the 12.6% month-over-month revenue drop despite a 41.5% rise in units.", "expected_impact": "A large unit increase with falling revenue signals a per-order value problem that needs prompt attention.", "evidence_ids": ["trend:revenue (B):MoM", "trend:units (B):MoM"]}, {"action": "Restore or collect the notes column, which is missing in over 99.9% of rows, befo